.\" dtrandr(1) - CDE display setup
.TH dtrandr 1 "October 2026" "CDE 2.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dtrandr \- apply the saved display setup that matches the connected monitors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dtrandr
.RB [ \-c | \-\-change ]
.RB [ \-\-default
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B dtrandr
.RB [ \-l ]
.I name
.br
.B dtrandr
.BR \-s | \-r
.I name
.RB [ \-\-system ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.br
.B dtrandr
.BR \-\-list | \-\-detected | \-\-current | \-\-fingerprint | \-\-config
.br
.B dtrandr
.BR \-d | \-\-daemon
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dtrandr
sets up the screens of an X display with the RandR extension.
It reads which monitors are connected (each with its EDID, the
identification a monitor sends), looks for a saved
.I profile
made for those monitors, and applies it: which outputs are on, at which
mode, rate, position, rotation, scale and so on.
It is the CDE counterpart of
.BR autorandr ,
and reads and writes autorandr's profiles, so that profiles saved with
either work with both.
.PP
The desktop runs it twice: the login screen
.RI ( Xsetup )
runs
.B dtrandr \-\-change
with the system profiles, and the session
.RI ( Xsession )
runs
.B dtrandr \-\-change
before the session manager starts, then
.B dtrandr \-\-daemon
to follow monitors that are plugged in or out while the session runs.
.PP
To make a profile, arrange the screens (with
.BR xrandr (1)
or another tool) and save the arrangement:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xrandr \-\-output HDMI\-1 \-\-auto \-\-left\-of eDP\-1 \-\-primary
dtrandr \-\-save docked
.fi
.RE
.PP
From then on, whenever the same monitors are connected,
.B dtrandr
applies
.IR docked .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-change
Apply the profile that matches the connected monitors, if it is not
applied already.  This is what
.B dtrandr
does without options.
If no profile matches, the profile named by
.BR \-\-default ,
else the
.B default
setting (see
.BR FILES ),
else the virtual profile
.B auto
is applied; a default of
.B none
leaves the display alone.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-load " \fIname\fP"
Apply profile (or virtual profile)
.IR name ,
whether it matches or not.  Every output it turns on must be connected.
A
.I name
without an option means the same.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-save " \fIname\fP"
Save the current setup as profile
.I name
in the user's profile directory.  An existing profile is only replaced
with
.BR \-\-force .
The names of the virtual profiles cannot be used.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remove " \fIname\fP"
Remove profile
.I name
from the user's profile directory.
.TP
.B \-\-system
With
.B \-\-save
or
.BR \-\-remove :
use the system profile directory instead (which takes write access to it).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Replace a profile with
.BR \-\-save ;
apply a profile even if it is applied already; allow
.BR "\-\-load off" .
.TP
.BI \-\-default " name"
The profile to apply when no profile matches (instead of the
.B default
setting).
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the profiles with the directory they are in, marking those that
match the connected monitors
.RB ( (detected) )
and those that are applied
.RB ( (current) ).
.TP
.B \-\-detected
Print the names of the profiles that match the connected monitors, the
best match first.
.TP
.B \-\-current
Print the names of the profiles that are applied now.
.TP
.B \-\-fingerprint
Print the
.I setup
lines of the connected monitors, as a profile's setup file has them.
.TP
.B \-\-config
Print the current setup as a profile's config file has it.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Apply the matching profile as
.B \-\-change
does, then stay and do it again whenever monitors are connected or
disconnected (or report another EDID), half a second after the last such
change.  Changes of the setup itself, its own included, are left alone.
It exits when the X server goes away (at the end of the session) or on
SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP.  Only one runs per display; another one exits
at once.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Change nothing and run no hooks: print the profile that would be applied
and an
.B xrandr
command line that does what
.B dtrandr
would do.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Say more, for example about keys of a profile that are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Say everything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is a directory, named after the profile, with two files.
.TP
.I setup
One line per connected monitor: the output name and the monitor's EDID
in hexadecimal.  A profile matches when its outputs are exactly the
connected ones and every EDID matches.  An EDID may be
.B *
(any monitor) or a pattern with
.B *
and
.BR ? ;
a profile with more exact EDIDs is preferred, then one whose patterns
spell out more of the EDIDs, then the first in the directory order below
and, within a directory, in name order.  A monitor that sends no EDID has
the fingerprint
.BI \-\-CONNECTED\-BUT\-EDID\-UNAVAILABLE\-\- output\fR.
.TP
.I config
For each output, a line
.BI output " name"
followed by lines of the options below.  Outputs the file does not name
are turned off.
.RS
.TP
.B off
The output is off.
.TP
.BI mode " name"
The mode, by its name or, if the output has no mode of that name, by its
size
.IR width x height .
.TP
.BI rate " hz"
Of the modes of that name, the one closest to this refresh rate.
.TP
.BI pos " x" x y
The position of the output's top left corner on the screen.
.TP
.B primary
The primary output.  Without it, the primary output stays as it is.
.TP
.BR rotate " normal|left|right|inverted"
The rotation.
.TP
.BR reflect " normal|x|y|xy"
The reflection.
.TP
.BI transform " a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i"
A projective transform, as
.BR "xrandr \-\-transform" .
.TP
.BI scale " x" x y
Scale the output's picture (a transform; a
.B transform
line wins over it).
.TP
.BR filter " nearest|bilinear"
The filter of a transform.
.TP
.BI panning " width" x height [+ x + y [/ track ...]]
As
.BR "xrandr \-\-panning" .
.TP
.BI gamma " r" : g : b
As
.BR "xrandr \-\-gamma" .
.TP
.BI crtc " n"
The CRTC to drive the output with, if it can; another one otherwise.
.TP
.BI dpi " n"
The DPI that sets the physical size of the screen (anywhere in the file;
without it, the DPI stays as it is).
.RE
.IP
Other keys (such as autorandr's
.BR x\-prop\- *)
are kept, but ignored.
.PP
A profile directory, and the profile directory as a whole, may also hold
programs that are run around a change (when they are executable):
.B predetect
before the monitors are read,
.B preswitch
before a profile is applied, and
.B postswitch
after it has been applied (not when nothing had to change), each also as
a directory
.IB name .d
of programs run in name order.  Those of the profile directory as a whole
run first, then those of the profile.  They get the environment variables
.B AUTORANDR_CURRENT_PROFILE
and
.B DTRANDR_PROFILE
(the profile),
.B AUTORANDR_PROFILE_FOLDER
(its directory) and
.B AUTORANDR_MONITORS
(the connected outputs, separated by colons).
.SS Virtual profiles
These are always there:
.TP
.B auto
Change as little as possible: outputs that are on and connected stay as
they are, outputs that are on but no longer connected are turned off,
and connected outputs that are off are turned on at their preferred mode,
right of the others.  If no output is on, as
.BR horizontal .
The primary output stays, or else is the first output that is on.
.TP
.BR horizontal ", " vertical
The connected outputs at their preferred modes side by side, or one
below the other, in name order; the first is primary.
.BR horizontal\-reverse " and " vertical\-reverse
take the reverse order.
.TP
.B common
All connected outputs show the same picture, at the largest size they
all have a mode of.
.TP
.B clone\-largest
All connected outputs show the same picture, at the size of the largest
of them; the others scale it.
.TP
.B off
All outputs off (only with
.BR \-\-force ).
.SH FILES
Profiles are looked for in these directories, and the first profile of a
name is the one used:
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dtrandr
(default
.IR ~/.config/dtrandr ),
where
.B \-\-save
saves, and where the global hooks are,
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/autorandr ", " ~/.autorandr
autorandr's (only read),
.TP
.I /etc/dt/dtrandr
the system profiles (in the CDE configuration directory, which is
.I /etc/dt
unless CDE was configured otherwise), where
.B \-\-save \-\-system
saves,
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autorandr
autorandr's system profiles (each directory of
.B $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
with
.I /autorandr
added).
.PP
Without a usable home directory (as when the login screen runs it), only
the system directories are used, and the global hooks are those of
.IR /etc/dt/dtrandr .
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dtrandr/settings ", " /etc/dt/dtrandr/settings
Settings, as
.IB key = value
lines;
.B #
starts a comment.  The user's settings win.  The only key is
.BR default :
the profile to apply when none matches, a virtual profile,
.B auto
(the default) or
.B none
(change nothing).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X display.
.TP
.B DTRANDR
When
.B no
(set it in
.IR ~/.dtprofile ),
the session does not run
.B dtrandr
at all.
.TP
.B DTRANDR_SYSTEM_DIR
Another system profile directory than
.IR /etc/dt/dtrandr .
.TP
.B DTRANDR_FAKE
For the tests: a file that describes a display, which
.B dtrandr
uses instead of the X server, and to which it writes what it applies.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (also when nothing had to change or no profile applies), 1
on errors, 2 for a wrong command line.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The profiles are autorandr's, and are written as autorandr writes them.
Not supported: autorandr's legacy profiles whose
.I setup
has MD5 sums of the EDIDs, the output properties
.RB ( x\-prop\- *,
kept but not applied), and the
.B \-\-skip\-options
and
.B \-\-match\-edid
options.
.B \-d
is
.B \-\-daemon
here, not autorandr's
.BR \-\-default .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xrandr (1),
.BR autorandr (1),
.BR dtlogin (1),
.BR dtsession (1)
